From: oneplus6t <https://github.com/briancappello/oneplus6t>
Subject: [PATCH] pm8998: allow writing the RTC

The qpnp RTC on this board free-runs from zero and cannot be set: the driver
only registers .set_time when the device tree enables writes,

    drivers/rtc/qpnp-rtc.c:
        if (rtc_dd->rtc_write_enable == true)
                rtc_ops = &qpnp_rtc_rw_ops;

so RTC_SET_TIME returns EINVAL and every cold boot starts at 1970-01-02. The
kernel still applies that value through CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS.

Droidian works around it with /usr/bin/timekeeper, which stores an offset in
/data/time/timekeep -- but /data is the Android userdata partition, so a
reflash wipes it and the workaround starts from nothing again.

Letting the RTC hold real time fixes it at the source: the hardware keeps
counting on battery, so hctosys hands userspace a correct clock at boot with no
offset file and no userspace correction needed.

fajita.dtsi already reopens &pm8998_rtc to set qcom,qpnp-rtc-alarm-pwrup, and
device tree overlays merge rather than replace, so this base value survives the
OxygenOS dtbo that the device boots with.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8998.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8998.dtsi
index 89499f864..6aec05a5c 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8998.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/pm8998.dtsi
@@ -119,7 +119,7 @@
 			compatible = "qcom,qpnp-rtc";
 			#address-cells = <1>;
 			#size-cells = <1>;
-			qcom,qpnp-rtc-write = <0>;
+			qcom,qpnp-rtc-write = <1>;
 			qcom,qpnp-rtc-alarm-pwrup = <0>;
 
 			qcom,pm8998_rtc_rw@6000 {
